====================================================================
DOMAIN: Hegel, Slavery, Haiti, and the Master-Slave Dialectic
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-27
PAPERS_FOUND: 19 total (High: 9, Medium: 7, Low: 3)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The debate over Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit and New World slavery centers on
Susan Buck-Morss's 2000 Critical Inquiry article (expanded into a 2009 book),
which argues that Hegel was reading reports of the Haitian Revolution in the
journal Minerva as he composed the lordship-and-bondage section (Phenomenology
Sections 178-196), and that actual chattel slavery -- not merely a philosophical
thought experiment -- was the historical referent of the dialectic. The thesis has
generated a substantial literature of both support and critique.

Three broad positions organize the debate. (1) Defenders of the Buck-Morss thesis
hold that grounding the dialectic historically in the Haitian Revolution rescues
Hegel's universalism: freedom's demand arises from actual enslaved people, not
from an abstract European subject. (2) Critics from within the Marxist tradition
(Heller) argue that the master-slave relation encodes class struggle within
European capitalism, and that Buck-Morss's postcolonial framing displaces
historical materialism without adequate substitute. (3) A third strand --
developed by Jaarte, Bernasconi, and Stone -- argues that regardless of the
historical genesis of the passage, Hegel's philosophy of history and his lectures
on Africa are irredeemably Eurocentric and pro-colonial; the dialectic cannot be
rehabilitated by its supposed Haitian inspiration because Hegel himself mobilized
the lord-bondsman relation to justify colonial slavery as "education."

A separate but related debate concerns the relationship between Hegel and Fanon.
Frantz Fanon's "The Negro and Recognition" chapter in Black Skin, White Masks
(1952) appropriates three passages from the Phenomenology but argues that the
Hegelian dialectic breaks down under colonial conditions of racialized
dehumanization: the Black subject is not recognised as a self-consciousness at all,
so the dialectical movement toward mutual recognition cannot begin. Recent
scholarship (Ng 2024, Badenhorst 2022) has revisited the Fanon-Hegel nexus with
close attention to both Kojeve's mediation of Hegel for the French tradition and
Sartre's influence on Fanon's phenomenology of race.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain is central to the overall research project because it tests whether
German Idealism's most influential philosophical structure -- the dialectic of
recognition -- is complicit with, indifferent to, or potentially illuminating of
the history of colonial slavery. The debates over Buck-Morss and Fanon directly
raise the question of whether the Hegelian system can be read as engaging with
actual colonial violence or whether its universalism systematically effaces that
violence.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The role of Kojeve's 1930s lectures in shaping how French (and Fanon's) reading of
the master-slave dialectic is thoroughly discussed, but Hyppolite's commentary
(which mediated Hegel for a generation of French philosophers including Fanon) is
underrepresented as a standalone object of analysis. Nick Nesbitt's book
"Universal Emancipation: The Haitian Revolution and the Radical Enlightenment"
(2008, University of Virginia Press) -- a major work arguing that the Haitian
Revolution enacted a more genuinely universal Enlightenment than European
modernity -- could not be directly verified via CrossRef and is thus excluded; its
absence is a genuine gap.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The synthesis should distinguish three registers: (a) historiographical claims
(did Hegel actually know about Haiti?), (b) textual-philosophical claims (does
the Phenomenology's dialectic encode or erase actual slavery?), and (c)
normative-political claims (can the dialectic be put to progressive anti-colonial
use?). These registers are often conflated in the literature, and keeping them
distinct will sharpen the analysis.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Buck-Morss thesis (pro-Haiti): 5 papers -- argues the Haitian Revolution
  directly informed the master-slave dialectic and should ground a universal
  emancipatory politics
- Fanon critique: 5 papers -- argues Hegel's dialectic presupposes mutual
  recognition that racialized colonialism structurally forecloses
- Pro-colonial Hegel: 4 papers -- argues Hegel's philosophy of history and
  lecture texts reveal that he used the dialectic to justify colonial slavery
  as "educative"; the Phenomenology cannot be disentangled from this
- Marxist/materialist critique of Buck-Morss: 2 papers -- argues postcolonial
  reading displaces class analysis without adequate replacement
- Mainstream Hegel scholarship (Pinkard, Pippin): 2 papers -- provides the
  canonical non-racialized readings against which the postcolonial literature
  positions itself
====================================================================
}

@article{buckmorss2000hegel,
  author = {Buck-Morss, Susan},
  title = {Hegel and Haiti},
  journal = {Critical Inquiry},
  year = {2000},
  volume = {26},
  number = {4},
  pages = {821--865},
  doi = {10.1086/448993},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Hegel composed the lordship-and-bondage section of
  the Phenomenology (1807) while reading accounts of the Haitian Revolution
  (1791-1804) in the journal Minerva, making actual New World slavery -- not a
  philosophical abstraction -- the historical ground of the master-slave
  dialectic. Buck-Morss contends that the dialectic's claim to universality is
  substantiated, not undermined, by this historical grounding: enslaved Haitians
  enacted the demand for freedom more concretely than any European revolutionary.

  RELEVANCE: This is the foundational text for the debate over Hegel and Haiti.
  Every subsequent paper in this domain either defends, refines, or contests
  Buck-Morss's historiographical thesis or her normative conclusions about
  Hegelian universalism. Essential for the literature review's treatment of
  whether German Idealism engages or erases colonial slavery.

  POSITION: Represents the "historicist rehabilitation" of Hegel's dialectic: by
  recovering Haiti as its historical referent, Buck-Morss attempts to show that
  Hegelian universalism was always already implicated in New World slavery, and
  that this implication should be acknowledged and built upon rather than
  suppressed.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, Haiti, master-slave-dialectic, Buck-Morss, universalism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{buckmorss2009hegel,
  author = {Buck-Morss, Susan},
  title = {Hegel, Haiti, and Universal History},
  publisher = {University of Pittsburgh Press},
  year = {2009},
  doi = {10.2307/j.ctt7zwbgz},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Expands the 2000 Critical Inquiry article into a book-length
  argument, adding a second part on "universal history" that situates the
  Hegel-Haiti thesis within a broader critique of Eurocentrism in the writing of
  world history. Buck-Morss argues for a genuinely global intellectual history
  that takes seriously the contributions of non-European events -- especially the
  Haitian Revolution -- to the formation of modern philosophical concepts.

  RELEVANCE: This book is the primary target of most critical responses in this
  domain, including those by Ciccariello-Maher, Heller, and Jaarte. Its
  methodological proposal for a counter-Eurocentric universal history connects
  the Haiti thesis to broader debates about the scope and orientation of the
  history of philosophy.

  POSITION: Extends the historicist rehabilitation thesis into a programmatic
  argument for rethinking "universal history" from the perspective of the
  colonized and enslaved.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, Haiti, universal-history, Buck-Morss, postcolonial, High},
  abstract = {Buck-Morss draws new connections between history, inequality, social conflict, and human emancipation through a fundamental reinterpretation of Hegel's master-slave dialectic. Historicizing the thought of Hegel and the actions taken in the Haitian Revolution, Buck-Morss examines the startling connections between the two and challenges us to widen the boundaries of our historical imagination},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{ciccariellomaher2014worse,
  author = {Ciccariello-Maher, George},
  title = {{`So Much the Worse for the Whites': Dialectics of the Haitian Revolution}},
  journal = {Journal of French and Francophone Philosophy},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {22},
  number = {1},
  pages = {19--39},
  doi = {10.5195/jffp.2014.641},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Accepts the Buck-Morss thesis that the Haitian Revolution
  informed the Phenomenology's dialectic but identifies a series of "ironic
  silences" in her account: she centers Toussaint L'Ouverture and neglects
  Jean-Jacques Dessalines, whose anti-identitarian violence was constitutive of
  the revolution's universality. Ciccariello-Maher argues, drawing on C.L.R.
  James's "The Black Jacobins," that the universal is not given in advance but
  forged through particular identitarian struggles -- and that Fanon's
  reformulation of the Hegelian dialectic (overlooked by Buck-Morss) is the
  corrective needed to think emancipation in "decolonized dialectical terms."

  RELEVANCE: This paper represents an important internal critique and extension
  of the Buck-Morss thesis that also bridges it to the Fanon literature. It is
  essential for showing that defenders of the Haiti-thesis do not form a
  monolithic camp.

  POSITION: "Decolonized dialectics" position: accepts historical grounding of
  the dialectic in Haiti but argues Buck-Morss's version is still too Hegelian --
  it needs to be radicalized via Fanon and Dessalines.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, Haiti, Fanon, Ciccariello-Maher, dialectic, universalism, High},
  abstract = {This article sets out from an analysis of the pioneering work of Susan Buck-Morss to rethink, not only Hegel and Haiti, but broader questions surrounding dialectics and the universal brought to light by the Haitian Revolution. Reading through the lens of C.L.R. James’ The Black Jacobins , I seek to correct a series of ironic silences in her account, re-centering the importance of Toussaint’s successor, Jean-Jacques Dessalines, and underlining the dialectical importance of identitarian struggles in forging the universal. Finally, I offer Frantz Fanon’s reformulation of the Hegelian master-slave dialectic—overlooked in Buck-Morss’ account—as a corrective that allows us to truly rethink progress toward the universal in decolonized dialectical terms.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{fischer2004modernity,
  author = {Fischer, Sibylle},
  title = {Modernity Disavowed: {Haiti} and the Cultures of Slavery in the Age of Revolution},
  publisher = {Duke University Press},
  year = {2004},
  doi = {10.1215/9780822385509},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Caribbean colonial slavery was not peripheral but
  central to the formation of Western modernity, and that the Haitian Revolution
  was systematically disavowed -- erased from historical consciousness -- by both
  European and American intellectual culture. Fischer analyzes literary, legal, and
  philosophical texts to show how this disavowal operated, and argues that
  recovering Haiti means rethinking modernity as structured by this foundational
  forgetting.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the broader cultural-historical framework in which the
  Buck-Morss thesis is situated. Fischer's concept of "disavowal" -- the active
  suppression of the Haitian Revolution's significance -- is a key explanatory
  resource for understanding why Hegel's possible engagement with Haiti was itself
  suppressed in the canonical reception of the Phenomenology.

  POSITION: Cultural-historical postcolonial critique that extends beyond the
  specifically philosophical debate over Hegel to the broader question of why
  revolutionary Haiti was systematically excluded from Western modernity's
  self-understanding.
  },
  keywords = {Haiti, modernity, slavery, disavowal, Fischer, postcolonial, High},
  abstract = {On 1 January 1804, the revolutionary slaves of Saint Domingue established the first independent black state in the Americas and proclaimed their break with the French Republic. After more than a decade of protracted bloody battles, the only successful slave revolution in world history ended. The richest sugar colony of the New World was reduced to ashes, and of the troops Napoleon had sent with genocidal intent only very few made it back home. But while the bicentennial of the French Revolution in 1989 and quincentennial of the "discovery" of America in 1992 were lavishly celebrated with acts of State, monuments, conferences, and polemics, the Haitian Revolution's anniversary is bound to be passed over in silence in both the halls of power and metropolitan academies. Although few would doubt the profound effect the slave revolution had on the Western Hemisphere, there has until now been no extended study of it, and some describe Haiti as unrelated to any of the worlds' major civilizations. Modernity Disavowed tells a very different story: the Haitian Revolution is at the core of Western modernity in the Age of Revolution, and one of the reasons for subsequent denial or silencing is that Haiti forced the recognition of this fact on slaveholders and imperial powers. At a time when racial taxonomies were beginning to mutate into scientific racism and racist biology, the Haitian revolutionaries recognized the question of colour and race as a political one and placed claims of racial equality squarely on the agenda. Yet, as the cultural records of neighboring Cuba and the Dominican Republic show, the story of the Haitian Revolution has been framed in terms of barbarism unspeakable violence, outside civilization, outside politics, and beyond human language. From the time of the revolution onwards the story has been relegated to the margins of history; to rumors, oral histories confidential letters and secret trials. Focusing on Cuba, the Dominican Republic and Haiti itself in the context of the African Diaspora, Modernity Disavowed argues that we cannot even begin to understand Creole cultures in the Americas unless we understand how they took shape around various forms of denial of the Haitian Revolution.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{fischer2010history,
  author = {Fischer, Sibylle},
  title = {History and Catastrophe},
  journal = {Small Axe: A Caribbean Journal of Criticism},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {14},
  number = {3},
  pages = {163--172},
  doi = {10.1215/07990537-2010-032},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: A critical response to Buck-Morss's "Hegel, Haiti, and Universal
  History" that raises historiographical and methodological concerns. Fischer
  acknowledges the importance of the Haiti thesis but questions whether connecting
  Hegel's dialectic to the Haitian Revolution actually serves the goal of centering
  Caribbean history, or whether it instead re-centers Hegel.

  RELEVANCE: This short response article represents an important corrective from
  within the postcolonial Caribbean studies tradition. It cautions against reading
  the significance of the Haitian Revolution primarily through the lens of what it
  means for European philosophy, raising the question of whether the Buck-Morss
  thesis is Haito-centric or Hegel-centric.

  POSITION: Critical complication of the Buck-Morss thesis from a Caribbean
  studies perspective, questioning the methodological priority given to Hegel
  over Haiti.
  },
  keywords = {Haiti, Hegel, Buck-Morss, Fischer, methodology, Caribbean, Medium},
  abstract = {Can we coherently conjugate high philosophy and slave insurgency under the heading of “universal history”? Is it possible to disentangle “universal history” from its roots in white supremacy and European imperial reason? Susan Buck-Morss' recent answer to that question, which was left somewhat unresolved in her seminal essay “Hegel and Haiti,” turns the Haitian Revolution and its half suppressed echo in Hegel's master-slave dialectic into an emblematic event. Universal ideas of liberty and liberation can only be glimpsed in singular moments of rupture, systemic break-down, and violence. The slave insurgency in Saint Domingue thus becomes a privileged moment in universal history. While I find Buck-Morss' reading of Hegel's master-slave dialectic in light of the Haitian Revolution more persuasive than many other recent attempts at thinking universality in relation to slave insurgency, I am troubled by the fact that Buck-Morss' anti-multiculturalist notion of universality is predicated on an epistemology of catastrophe and the violent stripping of the individual of cultural and social ties. If there is anything we can learn from the Western history of catastrophes, from Atlantic slavery to the Holocaust, it is that the nakedness and vulnerability of the victim is more likely to lead to fantastical cruelty than to the promotion of liberationist thought.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{jaarte2024colonial,
  author = {Jaarte, Miikka},
  title = {Colonial Slavery, the Lord-Bondsman Dialectic, and the {St Louis Hegelians}},
  journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {45},
  number = {1},
  pages = {43--64},
  doi = {10.1017/hgl.2024.3},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Advances two connected theses: (1) contrary to progressive
  defenders of Hegel, the lord-bondsman dialectic cannot be easily separated from
  Hegel's pro-colonialism, because Hegel himself used the dialectic to argue that
  colonial slavery temporarily educated its victims; (2) this pro-colonial reading
  of the dialectic, ignored by contemporary commentators, was in fact recognized
  and embraced by the St Louis Hegelians, who used it as a qualified defense of
  pre-Civil War American slavery. Draws on work by Bernasconi and Stone to
  establish Hegel's racism and pro-colonialism as central, not peripheral.

  RELEVANCE: Directly challenges the progressive-appropriation of the lordship
  and-bondage section (including Buck-Morss) by showing that Hegel himself
  applied it in a pro-colonial direction. The paper is essential for the claim
  that the dialectic is not available for emancipatory re-use without significant
  transformation.

  POSITION: Pro-colonial Hegel thesis: the lord-bondsman dialectic is internally
  connected to Hegel's defense of colonial slavery, not opposed to it.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, lordship-bondage, colonialism, slavery, St-Louis-Hegelians, Jaarte, High},
  abstract = {Abstract Hegel's lord-bondsman dialectic has been of especially great interest to progressive and radical Hegelians—broadly speaking, politically left-leaning interpreters of Hegel who object to certain social hierarchies and demand their abolition. They read Hegel as giving an account of how ‘lordship’ over others is an inherently unstable and unsatisfying social formation, even for its supposed beneficiaries. Marxists, feminists and post-colonial theorists have all found inspiration in Hegel's analysis of the lord and bondsman by applying it to concrete relations of oppression, such as capitalism, patriarchy, or racism. In contrast, recent scholarship on Hegel's views on race and colonialism has cast doubt on whether his systematic political philosophy and philosophy of history can be of similar use to progressives. Hegel was undeniably a Eurocentric thinker, but philosophers like Robert Bernasconi and Alison Stone have convincingly shown that he was also a racist and a defender of colonialism. Crucially, however, few of the scholars who write on Hegel's pro-colonialism have analysed this feature of Hegel's thought in connection with the lord-bondsman dialectic. In this article, I argue for two connected theses. First, I argue that contrary to the hopes of some progressive defenders of Hegel, we cannot easily separate the lord-bondsman dialectic from Hegel's pro-colonialism. This is because Hegel himself appealed to the lord-bondsman dialectic to argue that colonial slavery educated its victims, and could therefore be temporarily justified. Second, I argue that this pro-colonial reading of the dialectic, though largely ignored by contemporary interpreters, was in fact recognized and embraced by a group of Hegelians in North America known as the St Louis Hegelians. They used the lord-bondsman dialectic as a basis for a qualified defence of pre-Civil War American slavery.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{stone2017hegel,
  author = {Stone, Alison},
  title = {Hegel and Colonialism},
  journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {41},
  number = {2},
  pages = {247--270},
  doi = {10.1017/hgl.2017.17},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines Hegel's views on colonialism across his mature
  philosophical works, arguing that his defense of European colonial expansion is
  not a peripheral contingent feature of his thought but follows from core
  commitments in his philosophy of history and his doctrine of Sittlichkeit.
  Distinguishes between "moderate" readings that try to salvage Hegel's
  universalism and "radical" readings (associated with Bernasconi) that see his
  racism and colonialism as constitutive of his philosophical system.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most systematic philosophical treatment of Hegel's
  pro-colonialism from within Hegel scholarship. The paper is essential for
  situating the Buck-Morss debate within a broader assessment of Hegel's relation
  to empire, and for the distinction between internal and external critiques of
  Hegelian universalism.

  POSITION: Internal critique of Hegelian universalism: argues that colonialism
  is philosophically systematic in Hegel's mature work, not merely a biographical
  or contextual blemish.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, colonialism, racism, Stone, Sittlichkeit, philosophy-of-history, High},
  abstract = {Abstract This article explores the implications of Hegel’s Philosophy of World History with respect to colonialism. For Hegel, freedom can be recognized and practised only in classical, Christian and modern Europe; therefore, the world’s other peoples can acquire freedom only if Europeans impose their civilization upon them. Although this imposition denies freedom to colonized peoples, this denial is legitimate for Hegel because it is the sole condition on which these peoples can gain freedom in the longer term. The article then considers whether Hegel’s basic account of freedom can be extricated from his Eurocentric and pro-colonialist interpretation of the course of history. The article argues that matters are more complicated because that interpretation has significant connections with Hegel’s conception of freedom as self-determination.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{bernasconi2000racial,
  author = {Bernasconi, Robert},
  title = {With What Must the Philosophy of World History Begin? {On} the Racial Basis of {Hegel}'s {Eurocentrism}},
  journal = {Nineteenth-Century Contexts},
  year = {2000},
  volume = {22},
  number = {2},
  pages = {171--201},
  doi = {10.1080/08905490008583507},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Hegel's philosophy of world history is not merely
  contingently Eurocentric but that race functions as a structural precondition
  for the very beginning of world history in Hegel's account. The relegation of
  Africa and Africans to a pre-historical condition is not an empirical error
  correctable by more information, but follows from the logical structure of
  Hegel's historical teleology. Bernasconi shows that Hegel's racial schema in
  the lectures on world history systematically excludes non-European peoples from
  historical rationality.

  RELEVANCE: This is a foundational paper for the "pro-colonial Hegel" strand of
  scholarship and provides the philosophical argument that informs Jaarte's and
  Stone's subsequent claims. For the overall project on German Idealism and
  colonialism, Bernasconi's argument about Hegel's philosophy of history is as
  important as his work on Kant's race theory.

  POSITION: Radical critique of Hegelian universalism: race is structurally
  embedded in Hegel's philosophy of world history, not an incidental feature.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, race, Eurocentrism, Bernasconi, philosophy-of-history, colonialism, High},
  abstract = {(2000). With what must the philosophy of world history begin? On the racial basis of Hegel's eurocentrism. Nineteenth-Century Contexts: Vol. 22, Race and social theory, pp. 171-201.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{ng2024fanon,
  author = {Ng, Karen},
  title = {Fanon and {Hegel} on the Recognition of Humanity},
  journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {45},
  number = {3},
  pages = {571--597},
  doi = {10.1017/hgl.2024.25},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Defends an interpretation of Fanon's theory of recognition as
  revolving around a basic right to demand human behaviour from the other. Argues
  that Fanon develops key Hegelian ideas by employing a "concretely universal"
  concept of humanity (drawing on both Hegel's concrete universal and Sartre's
  existential humanism) as a normative orientation for what he calls a "world of
  reciprocal recognitions" -- equated with the creation of a "human reality."
  Analyzes three passages from the Phenomenology that Fanon cites in Black Skin,
  White Masks to reconstruct his theory of recognition.

  RELEVANCE: One of the most recent and philosophically rigorous treatments of
  the Fanon-Hegel relation. It is directly relevant to the question of whether
  Hegel's recognition framework can be reconstructed to address colonial
  dehumanization, or whether Fanon requires a break from Hegel.

  POSITION: Reconstructive appropriation of Hegel through Fanon: argues that
  Fanon develops a non-essentialist, concretely universal theory of recognition
  that builds on Hegelian foundations while addressing their colonial limitations.
  },
  keywords = {Fanon, Hegel, recognition, humanity, Ng, postcolonial, High},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
 This paper defends an interpretation of Fanon's theory of recognition as revolving around his claim that we have a basic right to demand human behaviour from the other. Developing key Hegelian ideas in a novel direction, I argue that Fanon's theory of recognition employs a concretely universal concept of humanity as a normative orientation for establishing what he calls a ‘world of reciprocal recognitions’, which he equates with the creation of a ‘human reality’. In the first section, I take up the three passages from Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit cited by Fanon in Black Skin, White Masks to outline three key features of Fanon's theory of recognition. In section two, I argue that there are three senses of ‘universal humanity’ operative in Fanon's work: a false universal, an abstract universal, and a concrete universal. Whereas the first two are critical, pejorative uses, the third provides the normative orientation for his account of recognition and social struggle. In the third section, I show how Fanon combines features of Hegel's concrete universal with features of Sartre's existential humanism in order to avoid an essentialist or ahistorical approach to human nature. Specifically, I argue that the ideas of self-transcendence and a universal human condition shed light on what Fanon refers to as the right of reciprocal recognition to demand human behaviour from the other, and our one human duty to not renounce our freedom.},
}

@article{badenhorst2022fanon,
  author = {Badenhorst, Daniel},
  title = {Fanon, {Hegel}, and the Problem of Reciprocity},
  journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {44},
  number = {2},
  pages = {321--344},
  doi = {10.1017/hgl.2022.1},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Contests the "shared-humanity" interpretation of Fanon's
  critique of Hegel's master-servant dialectic -- the view that Fanon objects to
  Hegel assuming that master and slave recognize each other as human. Drawing on
  Van Haute and a close reading of Kojeve, Badenhorst argues that Fanon's actual
  problem is with reciprocal struggle, not reciprocal humanity: the "French Negro"
  did not experience the cost and value of freedom through struggle in the way that
  Hegel's bondsman does, and the Kojeve-Sartre-Beauvoir commitments in Black Skin,
  White Masks illuminate this more precisely than the "ontological" reading.

  RELEVANCE: This paper clarifies the exact philosophical mechanism of Fanon's
  deviation from the Hegelian dialectic, which has implications for how the
  research project characterizes the relationship between recognition theory and
  the colonial condition.

  POSITION: Re-reading of Fanon's critique as targeting Hegel-via-Kojeve: the
  problem is not shared humanity but the absence of reciprocal struggle that the
  colonial situation forecloses.
  },
  keywords = {Fanon, Hegel, Kojeve, recognition, reciprocity, Badenhorst, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract In this article I put forward an interpretation of what is at stake in Frantz Fanon's claim that there is a reciprocity at the basis of G. W. F Hegel's master-servant dialectic. I do this by staging a critique of the ‘shared-humanity’ interpretation of Fanon's claim. Fanon's problem, as this interpretation understands it, is that the master-servant dialectic describes a situation in which two human beings knowingly confront one another as such. Such a situation—because human-to-human confrontation is assumed—does not adequately describe a racially divided situation because of racism's dehumanizing force. Fanon's problem would thus be that Hegel assumed shared humanity. I contest this reading by claiming that Fanon's issue is not reciprocal humanity but reciprocal struggle. To get to this point requires demonstrating that the shared-humanity reading is implausible on a variety of grounds. Thanks to the work of Philippe Van Haute it can already be said that in Hegel's text no shared humanity takes place. But Van Haute nevertheless claims that the shared-humanity problem is present in Kojève. Thus, it first needs to be shown that Kojève's text disallows such a reading. With this result in hand, I move to show the textually unsupportable nature of a subset of the shared-humanity reading—the ‘ontological reading’—whose strategy of reading Fanon's Black Skin, White Masks obscures the Sartrean-Beauvoirian commitments which would in fact allow a more accurate reading of Fanon's claims about reciprocity in Hegel to be made. I then seek to show—with reference to these commitments and a broadly Kojèvean emphasis on the centrality of struggle in human subject-formation—that Fanon's comments about reciprocity take aim at the question of mutual struggle in Hegel and Kojève. ‘The French Negro’, Fanon argues, did not get to experience the cost and value of freedom, unlike Hegel's servant.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{brown2024fanon,
  author = {Brown, Grant},
  title = {{Fanon}'s Postcolonial Critique of {Hegelian} Recognition and {Buck-Morss}' {Haiti} Thesis},
  journal = {Rhizomes: Cultural Studies in Emerging Knowledge},
  year = {2024},
  number = {40},
  doi = {10.20415/rhiz/040.e03},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Interrogates the relationship between Hegel's master-slave
  dialectic and Fanon's critique and reformulation of it in Black Skin, White
  Masks, using the Buck-Morss Haiti thesis as a contextualizing framework.
  Concludes that, while Buck-Morss provides a compelling historical grounding for
  the dialectic, her insights cannot fully vindicate Hegel from Fanon's rebukes:
  the Phenomenology still requires supplementation with a concrete analysis of the
  actual conscious experiences of racialized and colonized subjects.

  RELEVANCE: Represents a recent synthesis of the Buck-Morss and Fanon debates,
  arguing for their mutual illumination while maintaining that Buck-Morss's
  historicist rehabilitation does not dissolve Fanon's philosophical critique.

  POSITION: Synthetic position that holds the Buck-Morss and Fanon strands
  together while refusing to reduce one to the other.
  },
  keywords = {Fanon, Buck-Morss, Hegel, recognition, colonialism, Brown, Medium},
  abstract = {This philosophical investigation interrogates the relationship between G.F.W. Hegel’s concept of the master-slave dialectic in The Phenomenology of Spirit and the critique and reformulation of it by Frantz Fanon in Black Skin, White Masks. As a means of contextualization and expansion of Hegel’s original textual account, I consider Susan Buck-Morss’ seminal defense through grounding the dialectic in Hegel’s possible historical knowledge of the Haitian Revolution. I conclude that despite a compelling picture, Buck-Morss’ insights are unable to fully vindicate Hegel from the rebukes of Fanon, and as a result, Hegel’s phenomenology necessitates a concrete analysis of the actual conscious experiences of the racialized and colonized subject in order to realize its aims.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{bowman2023hegel,
  author = {Bowman, Jonathan},
  title = {{Hegel}'s Master-Slave Dialectic and the {Haiti} Revolt (1791--1804): Transatlantic Print Chronicles of Race in an Age of Colonial Market Exchange},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {51},
  number = {8},
  pages = {1209--1237},
  doi = {10.1177/01914537231211042},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Contributes to the transdisciplinary effort to view the Haitian
  slave revolt as the historical inspiration for Hegel's master-slave dialectic,
  arguing that asymmetrical racial classification schemes in transatlantic print
  culture (through figures like Gregoire and Du Bois, and archival records of
  French, British, and American parliamentary proceedings) show that European
  literate publics were well aware of the coercive forms of commodified labor in
  Saint-Domingue. Argues that recovering Haiti's proper world-historical warrant
  as inspiration for the dialectic need not lead us to overlook Hegel's own
  complicity with colonial racial ontologies.

  RELEVANCE: A historiographically detailed treatment of the archival evidence
  for the Buck-Morss thesis, situating it within the material history of
  transatlantic print culture and racial classification. Useful for the research
  project's treatment of how philosophical texts encode their historical
  conditions.

  POSITION: Empirically supported version of the Buck-Morss thesis that also
  acknowledges Hegel's continuing colonial complicity.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, Haiti, race, transatlantic, print-culture, Bowman, Medium},
  abstract = {This work contributes to recent transdisciplinary efforts to view the Haitian slave revolt (1791–1804) as the historical inspiration for Hegel’s master-slave dialectic. Reconstructions offered by contemporary postcolonial scholars argue that the Haitian revolt was chronicled in Minerva as Hegel raced to finish his Phenomenology. Benhabib recently recognized the Hegel-Haiti thesis as entailing the sort of inclusive dialogical learning process necessary to validate subaltern experiences. The thesis has also drawn its share of sceptical scrutiny as Badiou claims that it risks forcing an unnecessary moral dissymmetry, neglects objectionable features of enslavement and imposes a mismatch between apolitical subject and revolutionary. In reply, we appeal to the pioneering work of Tavares to show that the asymmetrical construction of classification schemes for persons of mixed-racial statuses accords with decades of documented literary exchanges between Hegel and Gregoire. We then turn to the work of James on the role of mixed-race merchants in the testimonial accounts of late 18th century French historians to show that European literate publics were well aware of the extremely coercive forms of commodified labour found in Saint-Domingue. We then invoke the archival work of Du Bois on French, British and American parliamentary proceedings that show the provisional colonial identities ascribed to Caribbean subjects did not hinder their self-conscious exercise of political agency. Viewing Hegel in terms of the historiographic records of the racial ontologies of early modern transatlantic literary exchanges helps explain how he adapted these tropes concerning mixed-race subjects in a manner that better explains many of the anomalous features of the dialectic. However, conferring the Haitian revolution its proper world-historical warrant as inspiration for his infamous master-slave dialectic need not lead us to overlook Hegel’s complicity with many of the epistemic and ontological flaws of colonial tropes held in early modern transatlantic print.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{goggin2024race,
  author = {Goggin, W. Ezekiel},
  title = {Race and Colonialism in {Hegel}'s Philosophy of Religion},
  journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {45},
  number = {2},
  pages = {185--209},
  doi = {10.1017/hgl.2024.9},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Offers a critical reconstruction of how race and coloniality
  shape the question of religion throughout Hegel's attempts to critique and
  vindicate European modernity. Paying special attention to the role of "fetishism"
  in Hegel's lectures on the philosophy of religion, the paper argues that Hegel's
  intellectual concerns are racialized from the inception of his project, and
  proposes an alternative philosophical approach to the concept of the "fetish"
  that resists Hegel's racist and pro-colonial tendencies.

  RELEVANCE: Extends the critique of Hegel's racism and pro-colonialism beyond
  the philosophy of history and the Phenomenology into the philosophy of religion,
  showing that the racialization of Hegel's thought is pervasive across his
  system. Part of the 2024 Hegel Bulletin themed issue on racism and colonialism.

  POSITION: Systematic critique of Hegelian racialization, focusing on the
  concept of fetishism in the philosophy of religion as a site where race and
  religion intersect in pro-colonial ways.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, race, colonialism, religion, fetishism, Goggin, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract Scholars have paid limited attention to the crucial relationship between Hegel's racism, his support for colonialism and his views on religion. This essay offers a critical reconstruction of how race and coloniality shape the question of religion (and vice versa) throughout Hegel's attempts to critique and ultimately vindicate European modernity. Paying special attention to the seminal role of ‘fetishism’ in his works, I argue that Hegel's intellectual concerns are racialized from the inception of his project. I conclude by suggesting an alternative philosophical approach to the concept of the ‘fetish’ and ‘fetishism’ to resist Hegel's racist and pro-colonial tendencies.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{james2024introduction,
  author = {James, Daniel and Knappik, Franz},
  title = {Introduction to Part 1 of the Themed Issue, `{Racism and Colonialism in Hegel's Philosophy}': Rationale and Topics},
  journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {45},
  number = {1},
  pages = {1--5},
  doi = {10.1017/hgl.2024.14},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Introductory essay for the Hegel Bulletin's 2024 themed issue
  on racism and colonialism in Hegel's philosophy. Establishes the rationale for
  the themed issue: it is increasingly recognized that Western modernity's
  progressive ideals (science, human rights, democracy) coexisted with brutal
  colonial conquest, and that pro-colonial ideologies shaped academic philosophy
  since the 16th century. The introduction maps the papers in the volume and
  identifies the central questions they address.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a useful state-of-the-field overview of 2024 scholarship
  on Hegel and colonialism, situating the individual papers (Jaarte, Goggin, and
  others) within a shared intellectual context. Useful for synthesis of the domain.

  POSITION: Editorial overview that does not advance a single thesis but maps
  the contested territory of Hegel's relationship to racism and colonialism.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, colonialism, racism, overview, Hegel-Bulletin, 2024, Low},
  abstract = {It is increasingly realized today that Western modernity has not only promoted progressive ideals such as scientific thought, human rights and democratic political systems. Its history is also marked by a much darker side, one of brutal conquest, biological and cultural destruction, enslavement and exploitation of non-European peoples in the context of European colonialism. This dark side of Western modernity was legitimized by pro-colonial ideologies of property, war, civilization, progress and race. Such ideologies emerged in areas like jurisprudence and philosophy since the 16th century, often building on views from classical antiquity (Pagden 1995). They shaped academic paradigms such as ‘scientific racism’, colonial anthropology and Orientalism in the 19th and early 20th centuries, and in many ways continue to influence contemporary societies and their academic practices.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{monahan2006recognition,
  author = {Monahan, Michael J.},
  title = {Recognition Beyond Struggle: On a Liberatory Account of {Hegelian} Recognition},
  journal = {Social Theory and Practice},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {32},
  number = {3},
  pages = {389--414},
  doi = {10.5840/soctheorpract200632318},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues for a "liberatory" reconstruction of Hegelian recognition
  that moves beyond the agonistic master-slave model. Monahan contends that the
  struggle-based account of recognition in the Phenomenology should not be taken
  as Hegel's final word, and that the more mature social theory of the Philosophy
  of Right contains resources for a non-agonistic account of mutual recognition
  that can ground anti-racist politics. Engages with Fanon and critical race
  theory as interlocutors.

  RELEVANCE: Represents the attempt to reconstruct Hegelian recognition theory
  for liberatory purposes in dialogue with the postcolonial critique. Its argument
  that "recognition beyond struggle" is available within Hegel's system stands in
  tension with Badenhorst's and Jaarte's more skeptical readings.

  POSITION: Reconstructive liberatory Hegelianism that argues for a non-agonistic
  account of mutual recognition as the core of Hegel's contribution to anti-racist
  politics.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, recognition, liberation, Monahan, critical-race-theory, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{heller2020hegel,
  author = {Heller, Henry},
  title = {Hegel, {Haiti} and Revolution: The Post-Colonial Moment},
  journal = {Third World Quarterly},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {41},
  number = {8},
  pages = {1442--1461},
  doi = {10.1080/01436597.2020.1763168},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Buck-Morss's claim that the master-slave dialogue
  encodes the Haitian Revolution is contested by Marxists who see it as expressing
  European class conflict. While agreeing with Buck-Morss that the Haitian
  Revolution critically affirmed the principle of universal freedom and pushed the
  French Revolution in a radical direction, Heller argues that Hegel's Eurocentrism
  and racism prevent his text from adequately affirming this universality. The
  alternative to Buck-Morss is not a refined Hegelianism but a Marxist historical-
  materialist analysis of the revolution's global significance.

  RELEVANCE: Represents the Marxist critique of the postcolonial rehabilitation
  of Hegel, arguing that the Buck-Morss thesis imports unnecessary idealist
  baggage when Marxist analysis provides a better account of the Haitian
  Revolution's universal significance.

  POSITION: Marxist-materialist critique of the Buck-Morss thesis: accepts the
  Haitian Revolution's universal significance but rejects Hegel (and postcolonial
  theory) as the appropriate framework for understanding it.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, Haiti, Marxism, Buck-Morss, Heller, historical-materialism, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract Susan Buck-Morss’s argument that the Haitian Revolution embodied the most universal aspect of the French Revolution, namely the quest for universal freedom, relies on the supposed references to Haiti in the master–slave dialogue in Hegel’s Phenomenology of Spirit. The revolution’s lodgement at the core of this foundational text of Enlightenment universalism is, for her, about as convincing a demonstration as one can have of the universal significance of the Haitian Revolution. Marxists have opposed her venture, and demonstrated their hostility to post-colonial thinking, principally by claiming that the master–slave is an expression of European class conflict. This paper agrees with Buck-Morss that the Haitian Revolution critically affirmed the principle of universal freedom and, indeed, pushed the revolution in France and Europe in a radical direction. A better affirmation of the universal significance of the Haitian Revolution than the thoughts of Hegel is possible. The latter do not actually provide such affirmation, because racism, Eurocentrism and a hostility to political radicalism are fundamental aspects of Hegel’s thought. The alternative affirmation can be found in Marxist analysis. This paper outlines such an analysis, and concludes that post-colonialism of Buck-Morss’s sort is no substitute for the perspective provided by historical materialism.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{basevich2022antiracist,
  author = {Basevich, Elvira},
  title = {What Is an Anti-Racist Philosophy of Race and History?},
  journal = {Critical Philosophy of Race},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {10},
  number = {1},
  pages = {71--89},
  doi = {10.5325/critphilrace.10.1.0071},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines what an anti-racist philosophy of race and history would
  require, taking Kant, Hegel, and Du Bois as case studies. Argues that both Kant
  and Hegel developed philosophies of history structured by racial hierarchy, and
  that Du Bois's critical appropriation and transformation of Hegelian themes
  (especially the phenomenology of double consciousness) offers a model for
  constructing an anti-racist philosophy of history from the ruins of its racist
  precursors.

  RELEVANCE: Bridges the Hegel-race literature with Du Bois studies and critical
  philosophy of race. By including Kant and Hegel alongside Du Bois, it connects
  Domain 4's Hegel-slavery debates to the broader project on Kant and German
  Idealism and colonialism.

  POSITION: Reconstructive anti-racist appropriation: treats Kant and Hegel as
  providing negative examples that Du Bois's philosophy of history critically
  transforms into an anti-racist alternative.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, Kant, Du-Bois, race, history, anti-racism, Basevich, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract:In this article, I defend the pragmatic relevance of race in history. Kant and Hegel's racist development thesis assumes that nonwhite, non-European racial groups are defective practical agents. In response, philosophers have opted to drop race from a theory of history and progress. They posit that denying its pragmatic relevance amounts to anti-racist egalitarianism. I dub this tactic "colorblind cosmopolitanism" and offer grounds for its rejection. Following Du Bois, I ascribe, instead, a pragmatic role to race in history. Namely, Du Bois argues that race is an "instrument of progress" that advances emancipatory struggle. He appeals to the writing of history—or historiography—to cultivate group consciousness of historical memory in order to (1) strengthen intragroup bonds among the racially oppressed, especially black Americans, and (2) create intergroup bonds that reconstruct the republic on the basis of universal ideals. I detail Du Bois's defense of the black struggle for freedom in the wake of the U.S. Civil War to provide a concrete illustration of "spirit" in American history.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{pinkard1994hegel,
  author = {Pinkard, Terry},
  title = {{Hegel}'s {Phenomenology}: The Sociality of Reason},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  year = {1994},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9781316036433},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Offers a systematic reading of the Phenomenology of Spirit that
  interprets the "sociality of reason" as the central claim of the work: for
  Hegel, self-consciousness is always already social, and the conditions for
  knowledge and action are constituted through social recognition rather than by
  an isolated Cartesian subject. The lordship-and-bondage section is read as
  illustrating the inadequacy of non-mutual recognition and the need for genuinely
  reciprocal social structures.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the canonical non-racialized reading of the master-slave
  dialectic against which postcolonial critics (Buck-Morss, Fanon, Bernasconi)
  position themselves. Useful for the literature review's presentation of what
  the Hegelian tradition took the section to be about before the intervention of
  postcolonial philosophy.

  POSITION: Mainstream Hegel scholarship: reads the master-slave dialectic as a
  philosophical argument about the social constitution of self-consciousness, with
  no reference to actual slavery.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, Phenomenology, recognition, sociality, Pinkard, mainstream, Low},
  abstract = {The Phenomenology of Spirit is both one of Hegel's most widely read books and one of his most obscure. The book is the most detailed commentary on Hegel's work available. It develops an independent philosophical account of the general theory of knowledge, culture, and history presented in the Phenomenology. In a clear and straightforward style, Terry Pinkard reconstructs Hegel's theoretical philosophy and shows its connection to ethical and political theory. He sets the work in a historical context and shows the contemporary relevance of Hegel's thought for European and Anglo-American philosophers. The principal audience for the book is teachers and students of philosophy, but the great interest in Hegel's work and the clarity of Pinkard's exposition ensure that historians of ideas, political scientists, and literary theorists will also read it.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{pippin2010hegel,
  author = {Pippin, Robert B.},
  title = {Hegel on Self-Consciousness: Desire and Death in the {Phenomenology of Spirit}},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  year = {2010},
  doi = {10.1515/9781400836949},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that the master-slave section of the Phenomenology is not
  primarily about social conflict or political struggle but about the structure of
  desire, the role of the life-and-death struggle in establishing self-consciousness,
  and the conditions under which genuine self-knowledge becomes possible. Pippin
  offers a "neo-Kantian" reading of Hegel that emphasizes the epistemological
  dimensions of the section over its social-political implications.

  RELEVANCE: Represents the mainstream analytic Hegel scholarship that reads the
  lordship-and-bondage section in purely philosophical terms, bracketing its
  historical connection to actual slavery. Understanding this reading is essential
  for the research project's assessment of why the Buck-Morss and Fanon
  interventions appeared as disruptions to a settled scholarly consensus.

  POSITION: Neo-Kantian analytic reading that treats the master-slave dialectic
  as primarily epistemological and structural, not historical-political.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, Phenomenology, desire, self-consciousness, Pippin, analytic, Low},
  abstract = {In the most influential chapter of his most important philosophical work, the Phenomenology of Spirit , Hegel makes the central and disarming assertions that "self-consciousness is desire itself" and that it attains its "satisfaction" only in another self-consciousness. Hegel on Self-Consciousness presents a groundbreaking new interpretation of these revolutionary claims, tracing their roots to Kant's philosophy and demonstrating their continued relevance for contemporary thought. As Robert Pippin shows, Hegel argues that we must understand Kant's account of the self-conscious nature of consciousness as a claim in practical philosophy, and that therefore we need radically different views of human sentience, the conditions of our knowledge of the world, and the social nature of subjectivity and normativity. Pippin explains why this chapter of Hegel's Phenomenology should be seen as the basis of much later continental philosophy and the Marxist, neo-Marxist, and critical-theory traditions. He also contrasts his own interpretation of Hegel's assertions with influential interpretations of the chapter put forward by philosophers John McDowell and Robert Brandom.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}
